---
// HELD: not published until Omkar confirms how the 960 labels were checked.
// To publish: rename to finbert-fusion.astro and set ready: true in src/data/projects.ts.
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import ThoughtProcess from '../../components/ThoughtProcess.astro';
import type { Metric } from '../../data/projects';

const metrics: Metric[] = [
  { value: '0.878', label: 'accuracy with confidence-gated fusion (FinBERT alone: 0.844)', evidence: 'measured', source: 'FinBERT fusion results, test n = 147' },
  { value: '12.2%', label: 'of test sentences escalated to the LLM (18 of 147)', evidence: 'measured', source: 'fusion inference output' },
  { value: '83.3%', label: 'LLM accuracy on those 18 hard sentences (FinBERT: 55.6%)', evidence: 'measured', source: 'fusion inference output' },
  { value: '5', label: 'sentences: the whole gain. Confidence intervals overlap', evidence: 'measured', source: 'bootstrap 95% CI, fusion 0.815–0.921 vs FinBERT 0.776–0.893' },
];

const facts = [
  { label: 'Type', value: 'Research' },
  { label: 'When', value: '2026' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Sole author: corpus, labels, fine-tuning, fusion, analysis' },
  { label: 'Data', value: '960 sentences from SEC filings of 6 companies' },
  { label: 'Status', value: 'Done · code being published' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'plain', label: 'In plain language' },
  { id: 'question', label: 'The question' },
  { id: 'obvious', label: 'Why the obvious fails' },
  { id: 'gate', label: 'The gate' },
  { id: 'system', label: 'Thought process' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'What came out' },
  { id: 'refuses', label: 'What it refuses to claim' },
  { id: 'open', label: 'Still open' },
];
---
<CaseStudy
  title="FinBERT fusion"
  question="When should a small model hand a sentence to an LLM?"
  description="Sentiment in SEC filings: a fine-tuned FinBERT handles most sentences, a confidence gate sends the unsure 12% to an LLM, and the result is reported with its confidence interval."
  crumb="Research · 05"
  links={[]}
  metrics={metrics}
  facts={facts}
  toc={toc}
  stack={['Python', 'FinBERT', 'Hugging Face Transformers', 'Claude (pre-labels, fallback)', 'SEC EDGAR']}
  prev={{ href: '/work/werkbank/', label: 'Werkbank' }}
>
  <section id="plain">
    <h2><span class="num">01</span>In plain language</h2>
    <p>
      Companies describe risks and results in long annual filings. This project sorts their sentences into
      negative, neutral and positive, and compares two sectors: oil and gas (BP, Chevron, Exxon) and enterprise
      software (Oracle, Salesforce, SAP).
    </p>
    <p>
      A small specialised model, FinBERT, does most of the work. When it is unsure about a sentence, a large
      language model gets that one sentence instead.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="question">
    <h2><span class="num">02</span>The question</h2>
    <p class="callout">
      Does sending only the sentences FinBERT is unsure about to an LLM beat FinBERT alone, and by how much,
      honestly measured?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="obvious">
    <h2><span class="num">03</span>Why the obvious approach fails</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>FinBERT alone</strong> is fast and cheap, but its weakest sentences are where it is most often
      wrong: on the sentences it was unsure about, it was right only 55.6% of the time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>An LLM on every sentence</strong> costs a call per sentence for a task the small model already
      gets right most of the time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="gate">
    <h2><span class="num">04</span>The gate</h2>
    <p>
      If FinBERT's top probability is at least 0.75, its label stands. Below that, the sentence goes to Claude
      Sonnet. On the 147-sentence test set, 18 sentences (12.2%) went to the LLM.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="system">
    <h2><span class="num">05</span>Thought process</h2>
    <ThoughtProcess
      steps={[
        { title: 'Collect', points: ['10-K Risk Factors and MD&A', '6 companies, 2 sectors', '960 sentences'], takeaway: 'Two sections where tone differs.' },
        { title: 'Label', points: ['LLM pre-labels', 'Reviewed before training', '426 neg · 413 neu · 121 pos'], takeaway: 'Labels before models.' },
        { title: 'Fine-tune', points: ['FinBERT, 3 classes', 'Stratified 70 / 15 / 15 split', 'Test n = 147'], takeaway: 'A small model that knows finance.' },
        { title: 'Gate', points: ['Top probability ≥ 0.75 stays', 'Below goes to the LLM', '18 of 147 escalated'], takeaway: 'Confidence decides who answers.' },
        { title: 'Fallback', points: ['Claude Sonnet', 'One sentence at a time', 'Right on 15 of 18'], takeaway: 'The expensive model only where it matters.', model: true },
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="results">
    <h2><span class="num">06</span>What came out</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Accuracy</th><th>Macro F1</th><th>95% CI (accuracy)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>FinBERT alone</td><td class="n">0.844</td><td class="n">0.822</td><td class="n">0.776 – 0.893</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gated fusion</td><td class="n">0.878</td><td class="n">0.875</td><td class="n">0.815 – 0.921</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The gain is real on this test set but small: 5 sentences out of 147. The two confidence intervals overlap,
      so this test set cannot prove that fusion is better in general.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the sector question: risk sections are about 70% negative in both sectors. The difference between oil
      and gas and enterprise software shows up in the MD&A sections, not in the risk factors.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="refuses">
    <h2><span class="num">07</span>What it refuses to claim</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>That fusion is significantly better.</strong> With overlapping intervals, the honest statement is
      "better on this test set, not yet proven in general".
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="open">
    <h2><span class="num">08</span>Still open</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Publish the code and small results (the fine-tuned model and corpus are too large for the repo).</li>
      <li>A larger test set, so the interval can separate the two methods or show they are equal.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
